{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.vm.ucf.holder.export\n  \"The exporting state of M-next D slice D8 (UCF 7.7.4; r3 1.8 and 1.9),\n   over the row carrier since phase 7b: the holder side of a lift, over a\n   machine value and data.\n\n   `enter` is the one-step transition into exporting: it sets\n   `:yin.k/gate :exporting` on the root, requires an empty ready queue,\n   and moves the wait set and every parked record into an export record.\n   The machine then has nothing to poll, resume or advance, and the gate\n   refuses what remains.  It refuses, as `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind\n   `:reason-mismatch`, a task that holds (root or any install child) an\n   `:observe` entry, any entry or cell carrying `:yin.k/held`, an\n   unminted cursor cell, a pending close, or a `:link-request` entry\n   whose response cursor is not installed.\n\n   `prepare` and `encode` are the split of r3 1.8, over `rows/lift-task`.\n   Prepare calls the exporter's `serve!` once per stream (recursively for\n   install children) and stores every answer in the record, keyed by\n   task path and resource id (the lift's `:serve-keyed`); `encode` is a\n   pure function of that record and the machine: it allocates and\n   publishes nothing, and the same record gives the same canonical pack\n   bytes.  The record retains the custody header and the composition's\n   source index the lift ran under: code travels by A, and every carried\n   A names its trees from that index (continuation §6.1).\n\n   `freeze` writes the export-recovery object, version 2: the published\n   pack (its address, its root id, its bytes) beside the holder-local\n   engine state the pack does not carry (gate, issue stamps, issued and\n   origin counters, per task) and the header, whose enrolled set never\n   travels.  The pack already carries the whole store, every parked\n   record and every module store the tasks reach, so no second body is\n   stored.  `rehydrate-fenced` restores it administratively through\n   `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` and proves the published pack is\n   reproduced byte for byte.\n\n   `abort` returns the record to the machine only when the offer cannot\n   have been admitted (7.7.4, r3 1.9).\n\n   Nothing here knows a transport, a driver or a journal.\"\n  (:require [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint :as checkpoint]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Refusal data\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- non-portable\n  [data]\n  (assoc data\n         :yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\n         :yin.k/kind :reason-mismatch))\n\n\n(defn- refused\n  [reason]\n  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/refused :yin.k/reason reason})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The tree of machines: the root and every install child\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- machines\n  \"`[path machine]` of `vm` and, recursively, each install child, in\n   install-name order of the map's own iteration.\"\n  [path vm]\n  (cons [path vm]\n        (mapcat (fn [[m inst]]\n                  (when (map? (:vm inst))\n                    (machines (conj path m) (:vm inst))))\n                (:installs vm))))\n\n\n(defn- held-entry?\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x) (contains? x :yin.k/held)))\n\n\n(defn- refusal-at\n  \"The first thing machine `m` holds that has no wire form, as the\n   data the refusal carries, or nil.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [waits (:wait-set m)\n        cells (filter map? (vals (:resources m)))]\n    (cond\n      (some #(= :observe (:reason %)) waits)\n      {:yin.k/hold :observe}\n\n      (or (some held-entry? waits) (some held-entry? cells))\n      {:yin.k/hold :held}\n\n      (some :yin.k/unminted cells)\n      {:yin.k/hold :unminted-cursor}\n\n      (seq (:yin.k/closes m))\n      {:yin.k/hold :pending-close}\n\n      (some #(and (= :link-request (:reason %)) (not (contains? % :cursor)))\n            waits)\n      {:yin.k/hold :link-cursor-not-installed})))\n\n\n(defn- first-refusal\n  [vm]\n  (some (fn [[path m]]\n          (when (seq (:ready-queue m))\n            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/not-quiescent\n             :yin.k/path path\n             :yin.k/ready (count (:ready-queue m))}))\n        (machines [] vm)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Entering exporting\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn enter\n  \"Enter exporting from `machine`.  Answers\n   `{:status :ok :machine m :record r}`: `m` is `machine` with the gate\n   `:exporting`, an empty wait set and no parked record, and `r` the\n   export record, holding the wait set in order, every parked record\n   (the row carrier carries them all, continuation §2.5), and the gate\n   to return to.  Otherwise answers the data outcome of the first\n   refusal, leaving `machine` as it was: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` for\n   queued work in the root or a child, a `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind\n   `:reason-mismatch` (naming the hold and the task path), or\n   `:yin.k/refused` for a machine that is already exporting or ended.\"\n  [machine]\n  (cond\n    (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine))\n    (refused :not-running)\n\n    :else\n    (or (first-refusal machine)\n        (some (fn [[path m]]\n                (when-some [h (refusal-at m)]\n                  (non-portable (assoc h :yin.k/path path))))\n              (machines [] machine))\n        {:status :ok\n         :machine (cond-> (assoc machine :yin.k/gate :exporting :wait-set [])\n                    (contains? machine :parked) (assoc :parked {}))\n         :record {:wait-set (vec (:wait-set machine))\n                  :parked (:parked machine)\n                  :gate (vm/gate-mode machine)\n                  :served {}}})))\n\n\n(defn- reinstated\n  \"`machine`, an exporting machine, carrying `record`'s waits and\n   parked records again: the value the lift reads.\"\n  [machine record]\n  (-> (reduce-kv (fn [restored module-name child-record]\n                   (update-in restored [:installs module-name :vm]\n                              reinstated child-record))\n                 machine (or (:children record) {}))\n      (assoc :wait-set (:wait-set record))\n      (update :parked merge (:parked record))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Prepare, then encode\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- check-header!\n  \"A header is nil (the fork lift) or a map carrying the arbitration,\n   the counter and the enrolled set; anything else is a defect of the\n   caller, not a refusal.  The lift judges the rest.\"\n  [header]\n  (when (some? header)\n    (when-not (and (map? header)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/arbitration)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/next-op-seq)\n                   (set? (:yin.k/enrolled header)))\n      (throw (ex-info \"Prepare of a malformed header\"\n                      {:yin.k/hint :malformed-header})))))\n\n\n(defn- lift\n  \"rows/lift-task of the record over `machine`, under the record's header\n   and source index, every stream asked of `keyed`: `{:status :ok :wire\n   w}` or the lift's refusal as data. A throw without the refusal\n   vocabulary is a defect and propagates.\"\n  [machine record keyed]\n  (try\n    {:status :ok\n     :wire (rows/lift-task (reinstated machine record) nil\n                           {:header (:header record)\n                            :source-index (:source-index record)\n                            :serve-keyed keyed})}\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n      (let [d (ex-data e)]\n        (if (and (map? d) (contains? d :yin.k/status)) d (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defn- seeded-by-handle\n  \"handle -> descriptor for every key the retained `table` already\n   answers, resolved through `machine`'s own resource bindings at each\n   key's task path: the alias reuse an interrupted prepare had already\n   proved, reconstructed before any new key is served, so a retry never\n   serves a handle the record already answers under another alias.\n   Handles are never persisted; this mapping lives within the one\n   call.\"\n  [machine table]\n  (let [at (into {} (machines [] machine))]\n    (into {}\n          (keep (fn [[[path id] descriptor]]\n                  (when-some [h (get-in (get at path) [:resources id])]\n                    [h descriptor])))\n          table)))\n\n\n(defn prepare\n  \"Prepare `record`: call `serve!` once per stream of the exporting\n   `machine`, install children included, and answer\n   `{:status :ok :record r}` with every answer retained in `r`, or the\n   data outcome of the lift's refusal with `:record` added, carrying\n   what was served before the refusal so a retry never serves a stream\n   twice -- not even under a resource alias the refused attempt never\n   reached, whose handle the retained table and the machine's bindings\n   still identify.  The row lift serves while it walks, so a refusal it\n   decides after the walk (`:op-seq-exhausted`, `:unprotected-pending`,\n   an op id on an unenrolled stream, the custody grammar) still finds\n   the streams it reached served and retained (phase-7a D4).\n\n   The served table is keyed by `[task-path resource-id]` and holds the\n   descriptor `serve!` answered. This table is plain data, but the live\n   waits can contain authentic values: `freeze` is the persistence seam.\n   A handle is asked at most once, across the calls of one retained\n   record, even when two resources hold it.  `header` is nil for the\n   fork lift, or the exclusive custody header (`rows/lift-task`'s\n   `:header`), kept in the record as `:header`; `source-index` is the\n   composition's `{A #{tree-address}}` (continuation §6.1), kept as\n   `:source-index`.  Prepare mints nothing: the occurrence was minted by\n   the driver before it was called.\"\n  [machine record serve! header source-index]\n  (check-header! header)\n  (let [table (atom (:served record))\n        by-handle (atom (seeded-by-handle machine (:served record)))\n        once (fn [k h]\n               (let [t @table]\n                 (cond\n                   (contains? t k)\n                   (let [served (get t k)]\n                     ;; an alias of an already-served key asks again for\n                     ;; nothing: the handle is known served from here on\n                     (swap! by-handle assoc h served)\n                     served)\n                   (contains? @by-handle h)\n                   (let [served (get @by-handle h)]\n                     ;; a handle already served under another resource id\n                     ;; is the one stream: no second call, and the table\n                     ;; must hold this key too or the record is unprepared\n                     ;; for a resource it reached\n                     (swap! table assoc k served)\n                     served)\n                   :else\n                   (let [served (serve! h)]\n                     ;; a refusal is not an answer: a retry asks again\n                     (when (some? served)\n                       (swap! table assoc k served)\n                       (swap! by-handle assoc h served))\n                     served))))\n        record (assoc record :header header :source-index source-index)\n        r (lift machine record once)]\n    (if (= :ok (:status r))\n      {:status :ok :record (assoc record :served @table)}\n      (assoc r :record (assoc record :served @table)))))\n\n\n(defn- kind-of\n  \"The lifted task's kind, by the lift's own rule.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [value (:value m)]\n    (cond\n      (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value))\n           (contains? (:parked m) (:id value))) :parked\n      (seq (:wait-set m)) :blocked\n      :else :halted)))\n\n\n(defn encode\n  \"The lift of the prepared `record` over `machine`, from a `serve-keyed`\n   that only reads the record, under the header and source index the\n   record retains: `{:status :ok :bytes b :address a :root B :kind k}`,\n   `b` the pack's canonical bytes, `a` the pack address (BLAKE3 of `b`,\n   the storage key), `B` the root id inside it; or the lift's refusal as\n   data.  It allocates and publishes nothing, and one record gives equal\n   bytes every time.  A stream the record has no answer for is a defect\n   of the caller (an unprepared record), not a refusal.\"\n  [machine record]\n  (let [served (:served record)\n        r (lift machine record\n                (fn [k _h]\n                  (if (contains? served k)\n                    (get served k)\n                    (throw (ex-info \"Encode of an unprepared record\"\n                                    {:yin.k/hint :unprepared-stream})))))]\n    (if (= :ok (:status r))\n      (let [wire (:wire r)]\n        {:status :ok\n         :bytes (cbor/encode wire)\n         :address (rows/pack-address wire)\n         :root (:yin.k/root wire)\n         :kind (kind-of (reinstated machine record))})\n      r)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Freeze: the export-recovery object, version 2\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- recovery-address\n  [bytes]\n  (let [algorithm jing/default-hash-algorithm]\n    (keyword \"segment\"\n             (str (get-in jing/registry [algorithm :address-id]) \"-\"\n                  (jing/digest-bytes algorithm bytes)))))\n\n\n(defn- recovery-metadata\n  \"The holder-local engine state of one task the pack does not carry:\n   the gate to return to, each wait's issue stamp in wait order, the\n   issued and origin counters, and each install child's, recursively.\"\n  [machine record]\n  {:gate (:gate record)\n   :origins (:origins machine)\n   :issues (mapv :yin.k/issue (:wait-set record))\n   :issued (:yin.k/issued machine)\n   :children (into {}\n                   (map (fn [[module-name install]]\n                          [module-name\n                           (recovery-metadata\n                             (:vm install)\n                             (or (get-in record [:children module-name])\n                                 {:gate (vm/gate-mode (:vm install))\n                                  :wait-set (:wait-set (:vm install))}))]))\n                   (:installs machine))})\n\n\n(defn freeze\n  \"The export-recovery object of the prepared record, version 2:\n\n     {:yin.k/export-recovery true\n      :yin.k/version         2\n      :yin.k/pack-address    a        ; the published pack's address\n      :yin.k/root            B        ; the root id inside it\n      :yin.k/pack-bytes      base64   ; the pack, byte for byte\n      :yin.k/header          h        ; nil for a fork; the enrolled set\n                                      ; stays here, never in the pack\n      :yin.k/state           {:gate :origins :issued :issues :children}}\n\n   Answers `{:status :ok :bytes rb :address ra :pack-bytes b\n   :pack-address a :root B :kind k}`, `rb` the recovery object's\n   canonical bytes and `ra` their BLAKE3 address, or the encode's\n   refusal.  Both objects must be stored durably before a fence is\n   recorded.\"\n  [machine prepared-record]\n  (let [e (encode machine prepared-record)]\n    (if (not= :ok (:status e))\n      e\n      (let [recovery {:yin.k/export-recovery true\n                      :yin.k/version 2\n                      :yin.k/pack-address (:address e)\n                      :yin.k/root (:root e)\n                      :yin.k/pack-bytes (jing/bytes->base64 (:bytes e))\n                      :yin.k/header (:header prepared-record)\n                      :yin.k/state (recovery-metadata machine prepared-record)}\n            bytes (cbor/encode recovery)]\n        {:status :ok :bytes bytes :address (recovery-address bytes)\n         :pack-bytes (:bytes e) :pack-address (:address e)\n         :root (:root e) :kind (:kind e)}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Rehydrate: the fenced recovery\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- invalid!\n  [data]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Invalid export recovery\" (merge {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable} data))))\n\n\n(defn- header-agrees?\n  \"The recovery's header is the custody the pack's root slot carries:\n   nil exactly for a fork; otherwise the slot's keys by kind (blocked or\n   parked: occurrence, arbitration, counter and origin; halted: origin),\n   a nil header value being an absent key, as the lift writes it.\"\n  [header baseline]\n  (if (nil? header)\n    (true? (:yin.k/fork baseline))\n    (let [ks (if (= :halted (:yin.k/kind baseline))\n               [:yin.k/origin]\n               [:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/origin])]\n      (and (not (:yin.k/fork baseline))\n           (= (into {} (filter (comp some? val)) (select-keys header ks))\n              (select-keys baseline ks))))))\n\n\n(defn- counter?\n  [x]\n  (or (nil? x) (and (integer? x) (<= 0 x))))\n\n\n(defn- valid-state?\n  \"The recovery state fits the decoded task: one issue stamp per carried\n   wait, and one child state per carried install, recursively.\"\n  [task state]\n  (let [ss (:slots task)\n        installs (nth ss 12)\n        waits (nth ss 13)]\n    (and (map? state)\n         (contains? #{nil :running :exporting :ended} (:gate state))\n         (vector? (:issues state))\n         (= (count (:issues state)) (count waits))\n         (every? counter? (:issues state))\n         (counter? (:issued state))\n         (counter? (:origins state))\n         (map? (:children state))\n         (= (set (keys (:children state))) (into #{} (map #(first (:slots %))) installs))\n         (every? (fn [inst]\n                   (let [[mn _ _ _ child] (:slots inst)]\n                     (valid-state? child (get-in state [:children mn]))))\n                 installs))))\n\n\n(defn- carried-trees\n  \"{A #{tree}} every task of the decoded tree carries: the composition's\n   index as the prepare that published the pack saw it, restricted to the\n   carried segments, which is all a relift of the same tree reads.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [ss (:slots task)]\n    (reduce (fn [acc inst] (merge acc (carried-trees (nth (:slots inst) 4))))\n            (or (:yin.k/trees (nth ss 10)) {})\n            (nth ss 12))))\n\n\n(defn- extract-recovered\n  \"The fenced machine and its export record from the lower's fenced\n   restore of one task: the waits and parked records the lower kept out\n   of the scheduler move into the record, the waits restamped with the\n   recovered issues; the holder-local counters come from `state`.\"\n  [machine state]\n  (let [children (into {}\n                       (map (fn [[module-name install]]\n                              [module-name\n                               (extract-recovered (:vm install)\n                                                  (get-in state [:children module-name]))]))\n                       (:installs machine))\n        waits (mapv (fn [entry issue]\n                      (if (some? issue)\n                        (assoc entry :yin.k/issue issue)\n                        (dissoc entry :yin.k/issue)))\n                    (:yin.k/recovery-waits machine)\n                    (:issues state))\n        record {:wait-set waits\n                :parked (or (:yin.k/recovery-parked machine) {})\n                :recovered? true\n                :gate (:gate state)\n                :served {}\n                :children (into {} (map (fn [[module-name recovered]]\n                                          [module-name (:record recovered)]))\n                                children)}\n        fenced (reduce-kv (fn [parent module-name recovered]\n                            (assoc-in parent [:installs module-name :vm]\n                                      (:machine recovered)))\n                          (cond-> (-> machine\n                                      (dissoc :yin.k/recovery-waits :yin.k/recovery-parked\n                                              :yin.k/custody :yin.k/closes :yin.k/issued :origins)\n                                      (assoc :wait-set [] :parked {} :yin.k/gate :exporting))\n                            (some? (:origins state)) (assoc :origins (:origins state))\n                            (some? (:issued state)) (assoc :yin.k/issued (:issued state)))\n                          children)]\n    {:machine fenced :record record}))\n\n\n(defn rehydrate-fenced\n  \"Restore a version-2 export-recovery object into fresh receiver-owned\n   values (UCF 7.7.4).  Before any attachment: the object's tag and\n   version (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`), its bytes against `address`\n   (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch`), the pack through the rows inspector (its\n   address against its bytes, the carrier, the custody grammar), the pack\n   root against the object's, the header against the root's custody\n   slot, and the state against the carried tree (any of these\n   `:yin.k/undecodable`, or the inspector's own refusal).  Then\n   `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` over `receiver`, `attach` once per\n   stream identity: every task stays exporting, its waits and parked\n   records outside the scheduler and in the record, its id counter\n   exact; no program code runs, no stream is read, written, minted or\n   closed, no install child initializes.  Never restores tenure,\n   custody, scheduler waits or a runnable gate.  Last, the recovered\n   record is prepared again from what was attached and must encode the\n   published pack byte for byte, or the answer is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   with `:yin.k/reason :recovery-inconsistent`.  Answers `{:status :ok\n   :machine m :record r}`, `r` marked `:recovered?` (abort refuses it).\"\n  [receiver recovery-bytes {:keys [address attach]}]\n  (try\n    (let [recovery (cbor/decode recovery-bytes)]\n      (cond\n        (not (and (map? recovery)\n                  (true? (:yin.k/export-recovery recovery))\n                  (integer? (:yin.k/version recovery))\n                  (= 2 (:yin.k/version recovery))))\n        {:yin.k/status :yin.k/profile-mismatch}\n\n        (not (jing/segment-bytes-match? address recovery-bytes))\n        {:yin.k/status :yin.k/hash-mismatch}\n\n        :else\n        (let [pack-bytes (jing/base64->bytes (:yin.k/pack-bytes recovery))\n              header (:yin.k/header recovery)\n              state (:yin.k/state recovery)\n              baseline (checkpoint/inspect (:yin.k/pack-address recovery) pack-bytes)\n              _ (when (:yin.k/status baseline) (invalid! baseline))\n              wire (cbor/decode pack-bytes)\n              task (rows/decode wire)\n              _ (when-not (= (:yin.k/root recovery) (:yin.k/root baseline))\n                  (invalid! {:yin.k/reason :root}))\n              _ (check-header! header)\n              _ (when-not (header-agrees? header baseline)\n                  (invalid! {:yin.k/reason :header}))\n              _ (when-not (valid-state? task state)\n                  (invalid! {:yin.k/reason :state}))\n              attached (atom {})\n              restored (rows/lower-task\n                         receiver wire\n                         (fn [descriptor]\n                           (let [identity (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)]\n                             (if (contains? @attached identity)\n                               (:answer (get @attached identity))\n                               (let [answer (attach descriptor)]\n                                 (swap! attached assoc identity {:answer answer :descriptor descriptor})\n                                 answer))))\n                         {:fenced true})\n              {:keys [machine record]} (extract-recovered restored state)\n              ;; each attached handle answers the descriptor it was served under\n              by-handle (into {}\n                              (map (fn [{:keys [answer descriptor]}]\n                                     [(:dao.stream/handle answer)\n                                      (select-keys descriptor [:dao.stream/identity\n                                                               :dao.stream/channel])]))\n                              (vals @attached))\n              prepared (prepare machine record #(get by-handle %) header (carried-trees task))\n              reproduced (when (= :ok (:status prepared))\n                           (encode machine (:record prepared)))]\n          (if (and (= :ok (:status reproduced))\n                   (= (vec pack-bytes) (vec (:bytes reproduced))))\n            {:status :ok :machine machine :record (assoc (:record prepared) :recovered? true)}\n            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable\n             :yin.k/reason :recovery-inconsistent}))))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) failure\n      (or (when (:yin.k/status (ex-data failure)) (ex-data failure))\n          {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Abort\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- not-appended?\n  \"True when `outcome`, the answer of an offer attempt's append, proves\n   the value was not appended.  `dao.stream`'s closed append outcome\n   set is {ok full invalid-value closed refused transport-error}: the\n   four refusals prove it; `ok` proves the opposite, and\n   `transport-error` stays the unknown-delivery refusal, as does no\n   answer.\"\n  [outcome]\n  (contains? #{:dao.stream/full :dao.stream/invalid-value\n               :dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/refused}\n             outcome))\n\n\n(defn abort\n  \"Return the exporting `machine` to local execution from `record`.\n\n   `attempts` is the offer-attempt history the composition persisted:\n   one map per attempt whose intent was durable before the send, with\n   `:append` the outcome its append answered (absent when none did).\n   Abort is legal when there is no attempt, or when every attempt's\n   append proves the value was not appended; anything else, including\n   an authority's refusal of one attempt, leaves the source fenced.  A\n   recovered record (`rehydrate-fenced`) is refused `:restarted`: a\n   restart never hands execution back.\n\n   A machine under custody (`:yin.k/custody`) is a holder exporting a\n   successor: it may abort only with `tenure`, `{:now n :bound b :live\n   true}`: `n` before `b` in one unit, and `:live` the ledger reader's\n   evidence that this lease is the occurrence's active lease at its\n   epoch.  A clock reading alone is not tenure.  After the lease has\n   ended its old local machine is never restored.\n\n   Answers `{:status :ok :machine m}` or `:yin.k/refused` data with\n   reason `:restarted`, `:not-exporting`, `:tenure-ended` (at or past\n   the bound), `:tenure-unconfirmed` (no ledger evidence) or\n   `:offer-possibly-accepted`.\"\n  [machine record attempts tenure]\n  (cond\n    (:recovered? record)\n    (refused :restarted)\n\n    (not= :exporting (vm/gate-mode machine))\n    (refused :not-exporting)\n\n    (and (contains? machine :yin.k/custody)\n         (some? (:now tenure))\n         (some? (:bound tenure))\n         (>= (:now tenure) (:bound tenure)))\n    (refused :tenure-ended)\n\n    (and (contains? machine :yin.k/custody)\n         (not (and (some? (:now tenure))\n                   (some? (:bound tenure))\n                   (true? (:live tenure)))))\n    (refused :tenure-unconfirmed)\n\n    (not (every? (comp not-appended? :append) attempts))\n    (refused :offer-possibly-accepted)\n\n    :else\n    (let [m (reinstated machine record)\n          prior (:gate record)]\n      {:status :ok\n       :machine (if (some? prior)\n                  (assoc m :yin.k/gate prior)\n                  (dissoc m :yin.k/gate))})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '153,295p' collab/phase7-plan.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '153,295p' collab/phase7-plan.md\"","aggregated_output":"- Two identities, each used where it belongs: the **pack address** `a = BLAKE3(pack bytes)` is the storage key and the ledger's `:yin.k/id` (a snapshot variant is an encoding, as today); the **root id** `B` is the semantic identity and enters the baseline as `:yin.k/root`. Two packs with one root are variants of equal baseline (impossible after sorting, but harmless). The offer request, the fence record and the report's `:yin.k/result` carry `a`; `offer-decision`'s `(jing/segment-address? (:yin.k/id fact))` holds for `a`.\n- The **rows inspector** (`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint/inspect address bytes`): address matches bytes (`jing/segment-bytes-match?`), bytes decode through `rows/decode` (every row hash, canonical numbering, frames), the root's custody slot by kind (the version-1 header rules of `checkpoint.cljc:183-232` on the slot's map; children nil), every op-id on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings in root and children against the root's occurrence, origin and counter (`check-op-id!` verbatim), duplicate ids refused, and the baseline `{:yin.k/kind :yin.k/root :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/origin :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/ops {op-id digest}}` where the intent digest is `BLAKE3(cbor [:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot])` with the payload **as the wire slot holds it** (`[:lit x]` or `[:row id]`, both canonical). A fork pack answers `:yin.k/fork true` as `inspect-v2` does. Every refusal is data; nothing throws (as today).\n- `admission/accepted-baseline` and `completion/successor-defect` read `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin` from the baseline, unchanged. `ledger/offer-defect` is unchanged (`:yin.k/kind` ∈ `#{:blocked :parked}`, the baseline compared whole).\n- Halted exit: the report's `:yin.k/result` is the halted pack's address; the terminal edge names it.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (inspector, sorted emit, fixtures regenerated, the three authority call sites, `ledger-v2.txt`).\n\n### 3.4 Crash cuts: which cuts are meaningful when the body is a Merkle row set\n\n**Finding.** The named cuts (`stage_e_world/cuts`) cut the progress journal around mint, serve, store intent/ack, two content-store puts (recovery first, body second), fence, offer; the authority's commit; the reply journal; and composition ticks. They are meaningful at the granularity of **one store put per object**.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Store the pack as **one** content-store object. Then every cut keeps its meaning: `:export/store-put-*` cuts the recovery v2 put, `:export/body-put-*` the pack put, and a pack is either whole or absent; a torn pack is impossible by the store's atomic insert. Storing rows individually (one object per row, Merkle-addressed) would make partial checkpoints possible and require a new cut family (`:pack/missing-row-before-fence`) and a new refusal path (`rows/decode` `:missing-row` at the authority), with no benefit in phase 7. Row-level storage is a phase-8+ option for deduplication across checkpoints and is out of scope. One new cut is worth adding cheaply: `:export/pack-put-after-recovery-missing` is already covered by `store-put-before` (recovery absent, nothing fenced); no addition needed.\n\n**Cost.** None beyond keeping the two-put order in `export-fence-step`.\n\n### 3.5 Publication of successors\n\n**Finding.** The protocol (`exit-mint` → prepare → freeze → fence → report → release → closure; the successor offer refused `:awaiting-completion` then admitted) is body-agnostic except for three values: the successor header (built from the machine's `:yin.k/custody`), the report's result address and the stored bytes.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No protocol change. The successor's header takes `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the machine's custody map as today; the lift writes it into the slot; the inspector's `:counter-regression` check compares slot against baseline. The report carries the successor's pack address; `reopen-exit` rebuilds from the journaled mint and the fence as today. The one wrinkle: `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` read the kind and occurrence from the stored bytes through the inspector; the rows inspector answers the same keys.\n\n**Cost.** Included in 7b.\n\n### 3.6 Authority and capability tokens versus sealed refs on rows\n\n**Finding.** Rows re-seal every stream and cursor-ref under the receiver's owner on lower (`engine/issue-ref`), authenticate refs on lift, and carry no credential (UCF 7.7.8: \"the envelope carries no credential; the holder is authenticated by the attribution the composition supplies\"). The owner ruled (2026-10-12) that ShiBi tokens are a configurable policy at the `:admit` seam, and (2026-09-27) that ShiBi is a tuple space emerging from index and query interpreters, with `dao.stream.remote` offering only a capability-agnostic seam.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; owner to confirm scope).** Phase 7 carries **no** capability token in any row and adds no row kind for one. Authority is ledger facts, attribution is the composition's resolver, tenure is lease evidence; sealed refs are the receiver's and are minted on lower. A token-bearing admission is ShiBi work at the admission seam, after phase 8.\n\n**Cost.** None.\n\n### 3.7 `:resume-mode` under version 2\n\n**Finding.** The owner's ruling (orchestrator log, 2026-10-11) is that the version-2 stack lane stays frozen and does not carry `:resume-mode`. Rows carry `:act` and `:tail` rows; a `:tail` is the register kernel's `:return-result` delivery and an `:act` the `:write-result` one (continuation §4.2, §4.3), so the information `:resume-mode` encodes is structural in version 3.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No work. Phase 7 does not touch version 2; version 3 needs no such key.\n\n### 3.8 Two gaps the brief did not list but phase 7 meets\n\n- **Continuation §11 Q4 (op-id rules on lower).** Closed by the rows inspector running inside `lower-task`'s custody stage for exclusive roots (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin, never the body's own occurrence). Fork roots refuse any op-id (`:fork-op-id`), as `inspect-v2` does. Decided, in 7a.\n- **Continuation §11 Q3 (install verification).** `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, exports and that the image loads, not link-id = parent nor image address = the manifest's. Adding both is about a quarter day in 7b. **OWNER decides** (§3.10 Q3); recommend yes, because the recovery object now trusts the pack for install children.\n- **Continuation §11 Q1/Q10 (the `:running` wire phase).** Unchanged by phase 7; Stage E's installer scenarios use phase `:parked` only. Leave open.\n\n### 3.9 Which kernels take part\n\nStage E runs the **semantic-register kernel only** (the reference lane). The de Bruijn kernels are covered by the cross-engine laws of the harness (61/350, accepted) and are not Stage E holders or sources; the holder driver never lowers through a layout. This follows the owner's matrix ruling and avoids three times the process matrix.\n\n### 3.10 The owner's questions (blocking)\n\n- **Q1, fork spelling in the custody slot.** nil (landed; recommended) or the draft's `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` (half a day of pin regeneration; the draft is amended either way).\n- **Q2, the REPL custody gap.** `yin.repl` composes the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel (`repl.cljc:144`) and wires `compose` (`repl/main.cljc:979-1011`). After phase 7 the driver reads only packs, so the REPL's custody path cannot lift its machine until phase 8 renames the semantic-register kernel into `yin.vm.semantic`. Options: (a) accept the gap until 8 (recommended: `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` is re-based on a semantic-register receiver, and the REPL's `:custody` branch is marked inert until 8); (b) pull the REPL's kernel switch into phase 7 (an extra slice, two to three days, touching `yin.repl` and its tests, which 4b/5 did not plan).\n- **Q3, the install checks of continuation §11 Q3** in 7b (recommended yes, a quarter day).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slicing\n\nEach slice: files, dependencies, acceptance tests, hosts, days (opus implementation + review rounds at the observed 2-4 codex rounds per slice, about half a day per round including fixes).\n\n### 7a: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures\n\n**Create.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` (the inspector of §3.3); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` (semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support`: `new-machine` over `sr/create-vm` with `tu/make-stream`, deterministic one-slot streams, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-installer`, `parked-linker`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `with-op-id`, `serve-table`, `header`, `source-index` capture through `linearize/project`); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (the five bases lifted by `rows/lift-task` under a header, the mutations as raw row edits with rehash where they touch a row and as root-slot edits otherwise, three anchors with full pack bytes); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` (the lift and lower custody rules of §3.1: header by kind, the three lift refusals, the seven grant checks with zero attachments, protection in both directions, exact counter restore, halted exclusive lowers ended, fork under `:exclusive` refused).\n\n**Change.** `rows.cljc` (`emit` sorts rows by id; `lift-task` `:header`; `lower-task` custody stage with `:grant`/`:protection`/`:exclusive`/`:fenced` opts; `check-custody!` replaced); `authority/grant.cljc`, `authority/completion.cljc`, `authority/admission.cljc` (inspector call); `ledger.cljc` (none expected; `:yin.k/root` in the baseline is opaque to the fold); `ledger_fixtures.cljc` (`park`/`halted` from rows packs; `path` → `ledger-v2.txt`), `ledger_fixture_test`, `crash_cut_test`, `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test` (fixture bodies only).\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing; owner's Q1.\n\n**Acceptance.** `rows_checkpoint_test` (every base accepted with its baseline; every mutation refused at its path; the file equals `render` on three hosts), `rows_custody_test`, `ledger_fixture_test` (every host rebuilds every frame byte for byte; the projection digest pinned), `crash_cut_test`, the eight authority tests green, `rows_test` and the safepoint harness unchanged (no root id moves), `store_write_audit_test` allowlist updated if the row table changes.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 2.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **4**.\n\n### 7b: the holder's export record, recovery v2, the driver's lower\n\n**Change.** `holder/export.cljc` (`prepare`/`encode` over `rows/lift-task` with `:header` and a `:serve-keyed` adaptor; `freeze` → recovery v2; `rehydrate-fenced` over `lower-task {:fenced true}`); `holder/driver.cljc` (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`, `reopen-exit`, `source-mint`/`exit-mint` unchanged in shape; `:export-version` removed); `compose.cljc` (`holder-config-keys`/`driver-config` drop `:export-version`); `holder/export_test`, `holder/driver_test`, `holder/writer_test`, `holder/reader_test` (machines from `rows_support`; rec1 rows replaced; the v2 fork-lift deftest deleted); `handoff_v2_holder_test` (one deftest deleted).\n\n**Depends on.** 7a; owner's Q3 for the install checks (a quarter day inside this slice).\n\n**Acceptance.** `export_test` (recovery v2 freezes and rehydrates fenced with zero program IO, the published pack reproduced byte for byte, aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved, install children rehydrated without initialization or IO, a restarted abort refused), `driver_test` whole (117 deftests), `writer_test`, `reader_test`, `evidence_test`; the law \"zero attachments before the custody stage refuses\" pinned by attach counts.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 3 implementation + 2 review (4 rounds; the driver is 2.9k lines and its tests 4.3k) = **5**.\n\n### 7c: the composition and the compose and fencing rows\n\n**Change.** `fencing_rows_test` (rows lift under header, `lower-task` with grant opts), `compose_test` (semantic-register `:receiver`/`:machine`; the plain-composition test per Q2), `compose_rows_test` (41 deftests; `offer-variant`-style helpers re-emit packs; content-store cuts see recovery v2 then the pack); `yin.repl/main.cljc` only if Q2 answers (b).\n\n**Depends on.** 7b; owner's Q2.\n\n**Acceptance.** All three files green on three hosts; the slow deftest `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` on each lane.\n\n**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **3**.\n\n### 7d: Stage E on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts, four process pairs\n\n**Change.** `stage_e_world.cljc` (`parked-reader-over`, `reading-writer` over `rows_support`; `holder-seams` supplies `:source-index`; `offer-variant!` re-emits; `snapshot`'s `:fences` reads pack digests), `stage_e_scenarios.cljc` (checks that name `:yin.k/id-counter` or body keys adjusted; row 1's \"two encodings\" assertion keeps its meaning), `stage_e_peer.cljc` (none expected), `stage_e_process_test.clj` (none expected; the Node peer build picks up the new namespaces), `stage_e_rows_test.cljc` (none expected).\n\n**Depends on.** 7c (the world port can begin on 7b and run in-process while 7c is in review).\n\n**Acceptance.** `stage_e_rows_test` on JVM, Node and Dart (slow tier, `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`); `stage_e_process_test` on the JVM over `[[:jvm :jvm] [:jvm :node] [:node :jvm] [:node :node]]`, every life its own process, every cut a SIGKILL, `target/stage-e-durability.edn` appended; the fast-lane gate `the-cross-host-handoff-across-processes-test` first. Row 10's canonical-byte agreement across hosts now holds for packs as well (sorted rows).\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart (in-process); JVM↔Node (processes).\n\n**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) + 0.5 for the slow matrix runs (the process matrix is about an hour per full pass on the remote hosts, per build-n-test) = **4**.\n\n### 7e: documentation\n\n**Change.** `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §2.5, §2.6 (custody slot by kind), §7.1-7.2 (the `:header` lift, the custody and fenced lower stages), §8.4 (phase 7 met), §10 (a 10.7 inventory), §11 (Q3, Q4 closed; Q1/Q10 left); `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5 (the custody table as ruled), §7 (pack address versus root id, sorted rows), §9 (the fenced lower); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.7.4 (recovery v2 paragraph replacing the version-1 one), 7.11.1 clauses 1-5 and 10 (stage letters updated); `docs/orchestrator-log.md` entry.\n\n**Depends on.** The owner's answers; concurrent with 7c/7d.\n\n**Days.** 1 + 1 review round = **1.5**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risks and what could force a redesign\n\n1. **The identity split (pack address versus root id).** If the owner or a reviewer insists the ledger's `:yin.k/id` be the root id, the content store contract must change (a verifier-taking put) or the authority must store under the pack address and record both; the second is what §3.3 recommends anyway, so the redesign cost is in naming, not mechanics. Sorting rows removes the host-order variance that would otherwise make JVM and Node packs distinct variants.\n2. **Fenced rehydration on the semantic-register kernel.** `rehydrate-fenced-body` relies on the version-0/1/2 lower's `:fenced?` arm (`handoff.cljc:3688-3699`, `:3722-3725`): gate `:exporting`, waits parked in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the FFI pair resources for `:ffi-request` pendings, `id-counter` carried exactly. `lower-task` has none of this; porting it is routine but the install-child case (children exporting, their parked records moved to the child's record) is exercised only by `export_test`. Medium.\n3. **Install children on the semantic-register kernel under the holder.** The harness proves rows 7-12 (4b stage 2) for lift/lower; the holder's reader (held observations, replay through child call ids) over semantic-register install children has no test today. If `reader.cljc`'s walk assumes a field the new kernel spells differently, 7b finds it. Low-medium: both kernels share `engine.cljc` for `:installs`, `:wait-set`, `:yin.k/held`.\n4. **The REPL gap (Q2).** If the owner wants the REPL's custody demo live between 7 and 8, phase 7 grows by a slice and touches the 4b/5 \"no REPL edit\" boundary.\n5. **Stage E's pinned row-5 behaviour** (\"the exposed gap marker is the first and only commit\", pending an Architect ruling) is inherited unchanged; a ruling during phase 7 would change `row5`'s check, not the transport.\n6. **Dart.** Stage E runs in-process only on Dart (as today); the numbering budget (`default-budget`) is far above Stage E's task sizes. The known Dart traps (reader whitespace, `for` over chunked seqs, multi-key `assoc` on nil, duplicate protocol params) apply to the new support namespace; the harness already avoids them.\n7. **Review load.** Phase 6b took three codex rounds on a 1.8k-line file; 7b touches the 2.9k-line driver through eight call sites plus `export.cljc` whole. Budgeted at four rounds.\n8. **What would force a redesign of rows:** a requirement that the body's identity be a byte address (contradicts Merkle rows; §3.3 resolves it with two identities); a requirement to carry lease or epoch in the body (contradicts UCF 7.2.1, not proposed); row-level storage with partial checkpoints (deferred, §3.4). None is live.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Order of work and concurrency\n\n```\n7a (custody, inspector, fixtures)        ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7b (export record, recovery v2, driver)  ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7c (compose, compose/fencing rows)       ──────┐   ├── 7d world port may start here\n                                               ▼   ▼\n7d (Stage E, in-process three hosts, then the process matrix)\n7e (docs) ─ starts after the owner's answers, lands beside 7c/7d\n```\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
